\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


A game\textquotesingle s classical value maximizes winning probability using classical strategies; its quantum value allows the specified entangled strategies. Parallel repetition plays three independent question instances and requires the source\textquotesingle s combined winning condition.

An $n$-qubit pure state is a unit vector in $(\mathbb C^2)^{\otimes n}$, considered up to global phase. A quantum algorithm applies permitted quantum operations and measurements; complexity depends on the gate and input-access model.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does the quantum value exceed the classical value for the threefold parallel repetition of Feige’s game? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2026/4}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
Feige\textquotesingle s game distribution, answer spaces, quantum correlation model, and joint acceptance predicate are not reproduced in the supplied question.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record open. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine whether entanglement improves the three-repeat value of the cited game.

